# Supabase (Postgres + Storage + Auth + Realtime) -- add your project's values here
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# Set to "supabase" once supabase/migrations/0001_platform.sql (and
# 0002_guest_seed.sql) have been applied to the project above. Until then, or
# with this unset, lib/data/adapter.ts serves the platform layer (profiles,
# posts, comments, likes, follows, signup) from the deterministic seed
# generator instead, so the guest demo works with no backend at all.
PEPIROS_PLATFORM_BACKEND=

# Drizzle connects directly to Postgres via the Supabase connection string
DATABASE_URL=

# --- LLM: two providers, one fast/strong tier each (lib/ai/client.ts). Groq is primary;
# --- Featherless is the fallback lib/ai/fallbackModel.ts reroutes to when Groq returns a
# --- 401/402/403/429 or a provider-marked-retryable error -- i.e. anything specific to Groq or
# --- this account on it (bad/revoked key, quota, rate limit), not a malformed request, which
# --- would fail identically on Featherless too. Either key works alone -- with only one set,
# --- that provider is used directly, unwrapped. Model routing per plan.md §3, adapted from
# --- Anthropic: a fast/cheap tier for archetype classification + high-volume leaf-generator
# --- fan-out, a stronger tier for pillar planning + the harder generators (methodology,
# --- statistical_validity, weaknesses).

# Groq (https://console.groq.com/keys). Every call in this codebase goes through generateObject
# (schema or enum mode) -- confirmed live that only openai/gpt-oss-20b and -120b support
# structured outputs on Groq at all, so those are the only two candidates for either tier
# regardless of rate limits (llama-3.1-8b-instant has a far higher daily allowance but 400s on
# any response_format: json_schema request). 120b > 20b on capability; both share the same
# free-tier rate limit (1K req/day, 200K tokens/day), so the fast/strong split here is about
# capability and latency, not quota.
GROQ_API_KEY=
GROQ_MODEL_FAST=openai/gpt-oss-20b
GROQ_MODEL_STRONG=openai/gpt-oss-120b

# Featherless (https://featherless.ai/account/api-keys), OpenAI-compatible, no first-party AI
# SDK provider -- see lib/ai/client.ts's use of @ai-sdk/openai-compatible. Its flat-rate "Chat
# Plan" ($25/mo) is contractually for interactive human use in Featherless's own UI, not
# programmatic app/API traffic, which is what this is; the metered Developer plan is the one its
# terms describe as intended for that. Defaults below were verified with a real authenticated
# chat completion against Featherless (200 OK) -- meta-llama's repos there are gated behind a
# HuggingFace org connection and 403 without it, so these two Qwen models were picked instead.
FEATHERLESS_API_KEY=
FEATHERLESS_MODEL_FAST=Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct
FEATHERLESS_MODEL_STRONG=Qwen/Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct

# OpenRouter (https://openrouter.ai, free signup, no card), the one
# vision-capable tier -- the `figures` generator's image-captioning call
# (issue #59). Neither Groq nor Featherless can serve this: Groq has no
# vision model at all, and Featherless's vision models returned
# capacity_exhausted on every attempt checked live. Verified with a real
# authenticated multimodal completion (200 OK, cost: 0) and a real
# structured-JSON response, both against the free `:free`-suffixed model
# below -- OpenRouter aggregates many providers under one key, so this one
# being rate-limited has real alternatives, unlike a single-provider host.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
OPENROUTER_MODEL_VISION=google/gemma-4-26b-a4b-it:free

# MCP token signing (mcp/server.ts, mcp_tokens table). Self-generated, not a
# third-party key: any long random string.
MCP_TOKEN_SECRET=

# Signs the app's own session cookie (lib/auth/session.ts). Self-generated:
# `openssl rand -hex 32`. Development falls back to a known constant so the
# demo works with no setup, but production refuses to start without one --
# signing sessions with a published constant would let anyone forge a login.
SESSION_SECRET=

# Google sign-in has no env var of its own: the provider is enabled inside the
# Supabase project (Authentication -> Providers -> Google), so the Google
# client secret lives there rather than here. See the README for the two
# dashboard steps. Sign-in degrades to an honest error until they are done.

# The app's own origin. MCP results have to carry a deep link back into the
# canvas (plan.md §7: Claude can't see the canvas, so text plus link is the
# whole interface), and nothing else knows what that origin is.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- Free, no account required. All three below are courtesy identifiers, not
# --- credentials: the APIs work without them, just in a slower shared pool.

# OpenAlex citation expansion (lib/services/citationExpand.ts). Supplying a
# mailto puts requests in the polite pool instead of the anonymous one.
OPENALEX_MAILTO=

# Crossref DOI resolution on ingest, same polite-pool convention.
CROSSREF_MAILTO=

# Semantic Scholar Related Papers rail (lib/services/related.ts). Genuinely
# optional: unauthenticated access is enough for a demo. Only worth requesting
# a key if the rail starts returning 429.
SEMANTIC_SCHOLAR_API_KEY=
